  8. WHAT THIS REPORT EXERCISES
  This class extends EasyMockSupport, so this report is part of the before picture for the mocking library
  whose floor advance carries the widest POM footprint in the whole migration: the installed release AND
  the intermediate release fail identically on the target runtime with an inaccessible object error raised
  from the library's own class proxy factory, and only a much later release succeeds. Fourteen of this
  module's sixteen unit test classes reference that library, so this folder is one of the largest before
  pictures the migration holds for that single upgrade.
  Every assertion in this class is unmodified by the migration. Its two test methods assert that the
  document review workflow returns created tasks whose reviewed document keeps its original identifier,
  and that starting the same workflow with no document selected raises the task plugin's own exception
  carrying its original message. Both assertions stand exactly as they stood at the base commit.
  The class exercises the workflow engine's client side API through mocks rather than the engine itself:
  the task data access collaborator is a mock and the process start call is an expectation on it. The
  engine's version does NOT change in this migration. It is the oldest load bearing engine in the reactor,
  drives 36 live process definitions, and could not be bootstrapped for testing without a database, so it
  is classified as residual unverified risk and assigned to the deployment and smoke gates rather than
  declared safe. This archive preserves that distinction rather than blurring it. Three findings bound the
  concern without eliminating it: the process definition corpus contains zero script tasks and zero script
  format declarations, so the engine never asks for a scripting engine and the platform's removal of the
  bundled script engine cannot reach it; its persistence layer reflects over the application's own domain
  classes through ordinary unrestricted reflection; and it reads no class bytes and touches no platform
  internal packages. A version bump without a demonstrated incompatibility is out of scope just as firmly
  as an unjustified omission, which is why the engine keeps the version it had.
  The module declares junit, spring-test, h2 and easymock as its testing dependencies, every one of them
  taking its version from the root aggregator and none of them hardcoding a version, and zero static
  mocking or bytecode mocking frameworks; a fifth test scoped dependency, jmimemagic, is a MIME detection
  library rather than a testing framework. The module appears in neither of the migration's two mocking
  framework removal lists. Consequently no test rewrite touches this module, and its POM changes not at
  all: comparing the module directory between the base commit and the migrated revision reports no change
  to the POM and no change to the test source.

