  10. HONEST LIMITS OF THIS EVIDENCE
  Five integration classes in this module produce NO file in this folder: BuckslipActivitiIT,
  BuckslipArkcaseIT, CandidateGroupIT, TaskActivitiIT and TaskDiagramIT, re-derived with
  find acm-plugins/acm-default-plugins/acm-task-plugin/src/test/java -name '*IT.java'.
  A unit test run stops at the test phase, while the integration test runner's goals bind later: the root
  aggregator declares that runner with no explicit phase on its execution, so both of its goals take
  their default phases, and both of those fall after the test phase. An absent integration test is
  neither a pass nor a failure and may NEVER justify a test exclusion.
  Those five classes are precisely the ones that exercise the workflow engine end to end, and this
  module's most engine proximate unit test, the workflow engine data access test, is dormant at the
  baseline under a pre-existing class level ignore annotation. Therefore the unit test evidence in this
  folder does NOT clear the workflow engine residual risk. That risk is assigned to the deployment and
  smoke gates; this folder supplies the module level before picture only, and overstating it would amount
  to a false clearance. The two mocked service layer test cases inventoried below must not be read as
  engine coverage, and a fabricated pass would have been worse still, since it would appear to clear a
  risk the migration deliberately left unverified.
  Because that engine version is constant, and because this module's source and POM are untouched, any
  difference between this report and its migrated counterpart points at the runtime itself or at a root
  level library change: the mocking library discussed above, the Spring test runner used by 11 of the 16
  classes, or the expression language library on the decision table path, whose accessor optimizer
  failure reproduced even against a bean compiled at the JDK 8 target and was therefore a defect in that
  library's own generated code rather than in the classes it reads.

  11. TWO FALSE POSITIVES A REVIEWER SHOULD NOT DRAW
  First, this reactor contains NO test retry tooling. A recursive search of all 145 POMs for a rerun count
  setting returns zero, and no retry plugin is declared anywhere, so a failure observed at the baseline is
  real and reproducible, and failing fast is safe. The reactor does carry a Spring retry APPLICATION
  library, declared as a root version property and consumed by exactly three child modules, which has
  nothing whatever to do with rerunning tests. In a task and workflow module, where retry semantics are
  part of the domain vocabulary, that inference is especially tempting and especially wrong; the class
  inventoried here carries no retry behaviour of any kind.
  Second, coverage exclusions are NOT test exclusions. The root coverage plugin configuration excludes
  eight package globs from coverage MEASUREMENT only, and disables no test. Bindingly related: the
  coverage agent hands its configuration to the test runner through the default argument line property
  export, so the pinned unit test runner declaration must NOT set a literal argument line element.
  Setting one would silently sever instrumentation and leave the coverage check goal evaluating an empty
  data set, which reports a pass while measuring nothing. Instrumentation must be verifiably live. The
  pinned declaration in the root aggregator was inspected and carries no such element, and no POM in the
  reactor carries one.

